{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>A page you never meant to be clickable, embedded invisibly elsewhere</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A page with no defense against being framed can be embedded inside a nearly-invisible <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> on an attacker's site. The attacker layers their own decoy content underneath it, a big "Claim your free prize" button, say, directly beneath the real, transparent frame. The user sees the decoy and thinks they're clicking it; the click actually lands on the real page's button on top, one they never saw and never meant to press.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      This works because a browser delivers a click event to whatever element is topmost at that screen coordinate at the moment of the click, regardless of what's visible to the human eye. An attacker exploits that by making the sensitive target frame nearly transparent and stacking it above their own decoy UI, so what the user perceives, the decoy, and what actually receives the click, the hidden frame, are two different elements occupying the same pixels.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is a framing problem, not a same-origin-policy problem, which matters for reasoning about it correctly: clickjacking doesn't read or steal any data across origins, the attacker never sees the framed page's content. They misdirect a real, authenticated click the victim's own browser is willing to send, cookies and session included, to a page the victim is already logged into. That's exactly why it's dangerous for any state-changing action reachable with a single click while logged in: a "delete account" button, a funds-transfer confirmation, a permission grant.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>frame-ancestors 'self': tell the browser who's allowed to frame you</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The fix is a response header the target page sends: <code>Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'</code>. It tells the browser "only pages on my own origin may embed me in a frame." If any other site tries to put the page in an iframe, the browser simply refuses to load it there, the frame stays empty, and the whole attack has nothing to click through to.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>frame-ancestors</code> is the modern successor to the older <code>X-Frame-Options</code> header. Where X-Frame-Options only supports two meaningful values, <code>DENY</code> (nobody can frame this) and <code>SAMEORIGIN</code> (only the same origin can), <code>frame-ancestors</code> accepts a real source list: multiple specific origins, wildcarded subdomains, <code>'self'</code>, or <code>'none'</code>. A page that legitimately needs to be embedded by a handful of trusted partner domains can allow exactly those, something X-Frame-Options has no syntax for at all.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Both headers are worth sending together in practice: <code>frame-ancestors</code> for the browsers that support CSP Level 2, effectively all current ones, and <code>X-Frame-Options</code> as a defense-in-depth fallback for anything older or for tooling that only checks the legacy header. It's also worth remembering <code>frame-ancestors</code> doesn't fall back to a page's <code>default-src</code> directive the way most other CSP directives do, a policy of <code>default-src 'none'</code> alone still allows framing by anyone unless <code>frame-ancestors</code> is set explicitly.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>One missing header, one silent click away from disabled 2FA</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A security review flagged that a SaaS product's account-settings page, including its "Disable two-factor authentication" button, had no clickjacking defense at all. A researcher built a simple proof-of-concept: an iframe of the real settings page, opacity near zero, positioned exactly over a decoy "Continue to free trial" button on a throwaway page they controlled. Anyone logged into the real product who visited the decoy page and clicked "Continue" would silently disable their own 2FA.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The fix was a single header, added at the CDN and edge layer so every response from the app, not just specific routes, carried <code>Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'</code>. No application code changed, the button, the page, the session logic were all fine, the missing piece was entirely a response header telling browsers the page wasn't meant to be embedded anywhere else.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The team also kept <code>X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN</code> alongside it rather than removing it, not because <code>frame-ancestors</code> doesn't work, but because a small percentage of their traffic came through embedded corporate proxies and older WebView-based internal tools whose CSP support was inconsistent enough that the team didn't want to bet the fix on CSP alone. Belt-and-suspenders cost one extra header line and gave real coverage for the long tail.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

