{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>frame-ancestors doesn't fall back to default-src</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A CSP with <code>default-src 'none'</code> might look maximally locked-down, but <code>frame-ancestors</code> is one of the few directives that doesn't inherit from <code>default-src</code>. A page still needs to set <code>frame-ancestors</code> explicitly even if every other directive is already restrictive.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A separate, complementary defense is <code>Set-Cookie: ... SameSite=Lax</code> (or <code>Strict</code>) on session cookies, which stops the browser from sending authentication cookies on many cross-site framed requests, so a hidden iframe wouldn't be logged in to hijack in the first place even if a page somehow lacked <code>frame-ancestors</code>. Layering both is standard practice, neither alone covers every path.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Worth testing the header actually took effect rather than assuming a deploy applied it. DevTools' Network tab shows response headers per request, and a quick manual test, try framing the page from a scratch HTML file on a different origin, confirm the browser console shows the "Refused to display" message, catches the common failure mode where the header got set on the main HTML document response but not on an API or SSR edge case that also renders sensitive UI.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

